[method]
The paper used solution-state NMR resonance assignment and NMR titration experiments to examine interactions between S. aureus PBP4 and soluble peptidoglycan fragments or muropeptides. The approach involved assigning NMR resonances of PBP4, monitoring chemical-shift perturbations upon titration with peptidoglycan-derived fragments, and using the resulting NMR data to support a docking-based structural model of a PBP4:muropeptide complex. This is related to the proposed method because both use solution-state NMR to characterize peptidoglycan-derived peptide/muropeptide substrates, but the paper did not use selective 15N/13C labeling of individual pentaglycine glycine residues or glycine-optimized 2D HA(CA)CO experiments to assign hydrolysis cleavage sites.
[results]
The NMR titration results indicated that PBP4 did not interact with high-molecular-weight peptidoglycan or synthetic stem peptides, but did interact with larger soluble peptidoglycan fragments or muropeptides. The chemical-shift perturbation data supported a structural model of a PBP4:muropeptide complex. The provided sections do not report quantitative binding constants, kinetic rate constants, or unambiguous cleavage-site assignments for pentaglycine, so the outcome is qualitative rather than a direct validation of the proposed isotopic symmetry-breaking cleavage-site assignment strategy.
[context]
The experiments were performed with S. aureus PBP4 and the PBP4(S75C) variant, using soluble peptidoglycan fragments, muropeptides, and synthetic branched lactoyl peptides. NMR resonance assignment and titration were complemented by pull-down experiments, PBP4 activity assays with muropeptides or peptidoglycan from S. aureus SH1000, imipenem/native mass spectrometry assays, and X-ray crystallography of PBP4(S75C). The provided sections do not specify the NMR spectrometer field strength, exact titration concentrations, or detailed pulse sequences, and the NMR context is binding/structural mapping rather than real-time hydrolysis kinetics or isotopic symmetry-breaking in pentaglycine.
[end related work 1]

